---
import { pageDates } from "../page-dates.js";
import PageLayout from "../layouts/PageLayout.astro";
import { BAG_YIELDS } from "../lib/calc/concrete.js";
import { LAYOUTS } from "../lib/calc/tile.js";
import { DOOR_SQFT, WINDOW_SQFT, DEFAULT_COVERAGE } from "../lib/calc/paint.js";
import { GROUT_CU_IN_PER_LB } from "../lib/calc/tile.js";
import { FLOORING_LAYOUTS, COMPLEX_ROOM_EXTRA } from "../lib/calc/flooring.js";
import { MUD_GAL_PER_1000, TAPE_FT_PER_1000 } from "../lib/calc/drywall.js";
import { MATERIALS } from "../lib/calc/gravel.js";
import { DECKING_SPANS } from "../lib/calc/deck.js";
import { IRC, toFraction } from "../lib/calc/stairs.js";
import { BUNDLES_PER_SQUARE, ROOF_STYLES, UNDERLAYMENT_ROLL_SQFT } from "../lib/calc/roofing.js";
import { SQM_PER_SQFT, SQFT_PER_ACRE } from "../lib/calc/area.js";
import { UNITS, BRICK_WASTE_PCT, MORTAR_WASTE_PCT, BRICKS_PER_80LB_BAG, BLOCKS_PER_80LB_BAG } from "../lib/calc/masonry.js";
import { TRIM_IN as WP_TRIM } from "../lib/calc/wallpaper.js";
import { TRIM_IN as CARPET_TRIM } from "../lib/calc/carpet.js";
---
<PageLayout
  title="Methodology"
  description="Every formula, constant and assumption behind the Material Guides calculators, with where each figure comes from and how results are rounded."
  updated={pageDates("/methodology/").updated ?? pageDates("/methodology/").published}
>
  <p>This page lists every formula and constant the calculators use. The values shown here are read from the same code that runs the calculators, so they always match.</p>

  <h2>General rules</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Units:</strong> US customary. Lengths in feet or inches, areas in square feet, volumes in cubic feet and cubic yards, paint in US gallons.</li>
    <li><strong>Waste is added before rounding.</strong> Rounding happens once, at the end, and always up, so you are never told to buy less than you need.</li>
    <li><strong>Product figures are typical, not universal.</strong> When a number depends on a product, the calculator lets you change it and the page tells you to check your label.</li>
    <li><strong>Testing:</strong> each calculator is covered by automated tests with hand-worked examples. Tables on the site are generated from the tested code.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Concrete</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Slab: length (ft) × width (ft) × thickness (in) ÷ 12</li>
    <li>Round column: π × (diameter in ÷ 24)² × height (ft)</li>
    <li>Post hole: (π × (diameter ÷ 2)² − post width²) × (hole depth − gravel base) ÷ 1,728, all in inches</li>
    <li>Cubic yards: cubic feet ÷ 27</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Bag</th><th>Yield used (cu ft)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{BAG_YIELDS.map((b) => <tr><td class="num">{b.weight} lb</td><td class="num">{b.yieldCuFt}</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      <caption>Yields of standard concrete mix as printed on common US bags. Fast-setting and specialty mixes differ.</caption>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Tile</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Coverage per tile: (width + grout joint) × (length + grout joint) ÷ 144</li>
    <li>Tiles: area ÷ coverage per tile × (1 + waste), rounded up</li>
    <li>Boxes: tiles ÷ tiles per box, or area × (1 + waste) ÷ sq ft per box, rounded up</li>
    <li>Grout: joint length per sq ft = 144 × (tile width + tile length) ÷ (tile width × tile length), in inches; volume = joint length × joint width × tile thickness; one pound of dry sanded grout is taken to fill {GROUT_CU_IN_PER_LB} cu in, the low end of typical values, so the estimate leans high.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Layout</th><th>Default waste</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>{Object.values(LAYOUTS).map((l) => <tr><td>{l.label}</td><td class="num">{l.wastePct}%</td></tr>)}</tbody>
      <caption>Planning rules of thumb used by installers. You can override them in the calculator.</caption>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>Paint</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Walls: 2 × (length + width) × height − openings</li>
    <li>Door: {DOOR_SQFT} sq ft (a standard 3 × 7 ft interior door). Window: {WINDOW_SQFT} sq ft (a 3 × 5 ft average).</li>
    <li>Gallons: area × coats ÷ coverage. Default coverage: {DEFAULT_COVERAGE} sq ft per gallon, the middle of the 250–400 range printed on most interior paint labels.</li>
    <li>Buying: whole gallons plus quarts; three or more quarts round up to another gallon.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Square footage</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rectangle: length × width. Triangle: base × height ÷ 2. Circle: π × (diameter ÷ 2)². Trapezoid: (side 1 + side 2) ÷ 2 × height.</li>
    <li>1 sq ft = {SQM_PER_SQFT} m² (exact). 1 sq yd = 9 sq ft. 1 acre = {SQFT_PER_ACRE.toLocaleString("en-US")} sq ft.</li>
    <li>Board feet: thickness (in) × width (in) × length (ft) ÷ 12, using nominal sizes.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Flooring</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Boxes: area × (1 + waste) ÷ sq ft per box, rounded up. Cost is charged on whole boxes.</li>
    <li>Default waste: {Object.values(FLOORING_LAYOUTS).map((l) => `${l.label.toLowerCase()} ${l.wastePct}%`).join(", ")}; plus {COMPLEX_ROOM_EXTRA}% for rooms with closets, many corners or several doorways.</li>
    <li>The cutting-waste tables come from a row-by-row model: joints staggered at least 8 in, offcuts of 8 in or more reused, last row ripped to width.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Drywall</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sheets: (walls + ceiling − openings) × (1 + waste) ÷ sheet area, rounded up, walls and ceiling counted separately.</li>
    <li>Screws: sheets hung across the framing; 12 in spacing on ceilings, 16 in on walls framed 16 in on center, 12 in on walls framed 24 in on center.</li>
    <li>Joint compound: {MUD_GAL_PER_1000} gal per 1,000 sq ft of board (USG: about two 4.5-gallon pails). Paper tape: {TAPE_FT_PER_1000} ft per 1,000 sq ft.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Gravel, sand and soil</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Volume: length × width × depth (in) ÷ 12; cubic yards = cubic feet ÷ 27; tons = cubic yards × lb per cubic yard ÷ 2,000.</li>
    <li>Weights used: {Object.values(MATERIALS).map((m) => `${m.label} ${m.lbPerYd.toLocaleString("en-US")} lb/yd³`).join("; ")}. A compaction allowance (crusher run 15%, sand and topsoil 10%) is added for bases that will be compacted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Decks</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Rows: deck width ÷ (board width + gap), rounded up. Each row is filled with whole boards; offcuts of 2 ft or more are reused.</li>
    <li>Joists: deck length ÷ spacing + 1. Screws: rows × joists × 2.</li>
    <li>Board spans from IRC 2021 Table R507.7: {DECKING_SPANS.map((d) => `${d.decking} ${d.perpendicular} in (${d.diagonal} in diagonal)`).join("; ")}.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Stairs</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Risers: total rise ÷ target riser, rounded up; riser height = rise ÷ risers; treads = risers − 1.</li>
    <li>Stringer length: risers × √(riser² + tread²).</li>
    <li>Checks against IRC R311.7: riser ≤ {toFraction(IRC.maxRiserIn)} in, tread ≥ {IRC.minTreadIn} in, width ≥ {IRC.minWidthIn} in. Comfort rule: 2 × riser + tread between 24 and 25.5 in.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Roofing</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pitch multiplier: √(1 + (rise ÷ 12)²). Roof area = footprint × multiplier. Hip length per foot of run: √(2 + (rise ÷ 12)²).</li>
    <li>Bundles: squares × (1 + waste) × {BUNDLES_PER_SQUARE}, rounded up. Waste: gable {ROOF_STYLES.gable.wastePct}%, hip {ROOF_STYLES.hip.wastePct}%.</li>
    <li>Underlayment: one roll per {UNDERLAYMENT_ROLL_SQFT.toLocaleString("en-US")} sq ft (a typical synthetic roll). Drip edge: 10 ft pieces.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Mulch and soil</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Cubic feet = area × depth (in) ÷ 12; cubic yards = cubic feet ÷ 27; bags = cubic feet ÷ bag size (1.5, 2 or 3 cu ft), rounded up. A top-up counts only target depth minus existing depth.</li>
    <li>Depth guidance from Iowa State University Extension: 3 to 4 in of wood chips or bark on well-drained sites, 2 to 3 in on heavy soils, 6 in clear of trunks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Fences</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Sections = (length − gate openings) ÷ post spacing, rounded up. Posts = sections + 1 + one per gate. Rails: 2 per section up to 4 ft tall, 3 above.</li>
    <li>Pickets = fence line ÷ (picket width + gap) + 5%. Concrete per post uses the post-hole formula above, with a 6 in gravel base and 10% extra.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Brick and block</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Units per sq ft and mortar per 1,000 units (3/8 in joints) from BIA Technical Note 10: {Object.values(UNITS).filter((u) => u.mortarPer1000).map((u) => `${u.label} ${u.perSqFt} per sq ft, ${u.mortarPer1000} cu ft mortar per 1,000`).join("; ")}. Concrete block: 1.125 per sq ft.</li>
    <li>Allowances: {BRICK_WASTE_PCT}% extra units, {MORTAR_WASTE_PCT}% extra mortar (BIA suggests at least 5% and 15 to 25%).</li>
    <li>Bags: one 80 lb bag of QUIKRETE Mason Mix lays up to {BRICKS_PER_80LB_BAG} standard bricks or {BLOCKS_PER_80LB_BAG} blocks (data sheet). Other brick sizes are converted through mortar volume.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Wallpaper</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Strip length = wall height + {WP_TRIM} in, rounded up to whole pattern repeats; half-drop adds half a repeat. Strips per roll = roll length ÷ strip length, rounded down.</li>
    <li>Strips = (wall width − 3 ft per door) ÷ roll width, rounded up. Rolls = strips ÷ strips per roll, rounded up. Windows are not subtracted.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Carpet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Drops = room dimension across the roll ÷ roll width, rounded up; cut length = the other dimension + {CARPET_TRIM} in per end. Both directions are tried and the one using less carpet is shown.</li>
    <li>Pad = room area + 5%. Tack strip = perimeter − doorways.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Sources</h2>
  <!-- TODO: before launch, link each source below to the exact page you checked it against. -->
  <ul>
    <li>Concrete bag yields: <a href="https://www.quikrete.com/PDFs/DATA_SHEET-Concrete%20Mix%201101.pdf" rel="noopener">QUIKRETE Concrete Mix data sheet</a> and labels of standard concrete mixes sold at US home centers.</li>
    <li>Paint coverage: spread rates on labels and technical data sheets of interior latex paints from major US brands.</li>
    <li>Slab, footing and stair guidance: the <a href="https://www.iccsafe.org/" rel="noopener">International Residential Code (IRC)</a>, as adopted by your state or city. Stair limits checked against <a href="https://www.KnoxCounty.org/codes/pdfs/construction_info/Stairs.pdf" rel="noopener">a county summary of IRC R311.7</a>.</li>
    <li>Deck spans: IRC 2021 Tables R507.6 and R507.7, as printed in <a href="https://www.aacounty.org/sites/default/files/2023-03/Deck_Guide.pdf" rel="noopener">Anne Arundel County's deck guide</a>; <a href="https://awc.org/publications/dca/dca6/" rel="noopener">AWC DCA 6</a>.</li>
    <li>Drywall: <a href="https://assemblies-tools.usg.com/content/usgcom/en/resource-center/faqs/1977.html" rel="noopener">USG joint compound estimate</a>; screw spacing per ASTM C840 and GA-216 as summarized by <a href="https://wconline.com/articles/84977-all-things-gypsum-attachment-of-gypsum-board" rel="noopener">Walls &amp; Ceilings</a>.</li>
    <li>Gravel weights: <a href="https://selectsg.com/how-much-does-a-yard-of-crushed-stone-weigh/" rel="noopener">a quarry supplier's published figures</a> and typical supplier ranges.</li>
    <li>Shingles: <a href="https://www.gaf.com/en-us/blog/your-home/how-many-bundles-of-shingles-are-in-a-square---63a47862-f7e1-4e73-abcf-8d09917a8b8d" rel="noopener">GAF on bundles per square</a>.</li>
    <li>Brick and mortar: <a href="https://gobrick.com/media/file/10-dimensioning-and-estimating-brick-masonry.pdf" rel="noopener">BIA Technical Note 10</a>; <a href="https://www.quikrete.com/PDFs/DATA_SHEET-Mason%20Mix%201136.pdf" rel="noopener">QUIKRETE Mason Mix data sheet</a>.</li>
    <li>Mulch depth: <a href="https://yardandgarden.extension.iastate.edu/faq/how-deep-should-mulch-layer-be-around-trees-and-shrubs" rel="noopener">Iowa State University Extension</a>.</li>
    <li>Wallpaper roll sizes: <a href="https://www.selectedwallpapers.com/pages/european-rolls-vs-double-rolls" rel="noopener">Selected Wallpapers</a> and maker labels.</li>
    <li>Unit conversions: <a href="https://www.nist.gov/pml/special-publication-811/nist-guide-si-appendix-b-conversion-factors/nist-guide-si-appendix-b8" rel="noopener">NIST SP 811, Appendix B</a>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Corrections</h2>
  <p>If you find an error, please <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>. Confirmed corrections are made promptly and the page's updated date changes.</p>
</PageLayout>
